+\AuthorResponse{We thank the reviewer for these questions. The difference in question is the attention gain in the one-layer BiLSTM (0.2447 to 0.2429~mm\,h$^{-1}$). Using five matched seeds and paired comparisons on the 3,939 common test targets, the reduction was $-0.0018$~mm\,h$^{-1}$ ($-0.73\%$); all five seeds improved, and both the seed-t and moving-block intervals excluded zero. After Holm adjustment over the 16 all-target RMSE comparisons, however, the adjusted $p$ values were 0.17 and 0.13, and the wet and upper-tail differences were not clearly separated. We therefore no longer describe attention as a demonstrated benefit. On the second question, the added complexity is not justified by point accuracy alone in this setting. BiLSTM-Attention and XGBoost differed by only $-0.43\%$ overall, with both intervals including zero; XGBoost was better in the wet and upper-tail subsets; the three probabilistic recurrent models had 1.3--2.2\% higher overall RMSE than XGBoost; and adding attention to BU-LSTM gave no clear RMSE or CRPS improvement despite 159,272 additional parameters. The neural models also required about 105--393~s of recorded training per seed, compared with 0.1--0.4~s for XGBoost, although these timings are not a controlled benchmark and inference cost was not measured. For point forecasts in this setting, XGBoost therefore provided comparable overall accuracy with much shorter recorded training; because these run times are not a unified computational-cost benchmark, we do not claim a general efficiency ranking. The probabilistic recurrent models are useful only where predictive distributions are required, subject to their upper-tail undercoverage. These conclusions are restricted to this regional ERA5 target, lead time and set of configurations. The revised discussion is in Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:overall_discussion}, and the paired comparisons and recorded costs are in Supplementary Tables~\ref{supp-stab:r3sig_pairs} and~\ref{supp-stab:r3sig_cost}.}
 
